From d1a44b44fb90bcd214ece5037d96ef333718873b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jacob Root <me@rooty.dev>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 13:31:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vm_control: prefault virtio-nvgpu's window mappings

A guest's first touch of each page of a window mapping faulted out to KVM,
about 3.1 us a page for BAR1 video memory (a PFNMAP): a fresh 64 MiB
mapping was written at 1.3 GB/s against the host's 11. KVM_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY
(Linux 6.11) maps the same pages from inside the kernel, about 0.13 us a
page, but it is a vCPU's call and a running vCPU holds its lock in KVM_RUN.

So the main process makes one more vCPU that never runs (an id past the
ACPI tables', the supported CPUID, so the same TDP root), and a thread of
its own prefaults each window mapping the nvgpu tube registered, after the
mapping has been answered: a hint bounded to 1024 waiting ranges. A range
withdrawn before its turn is refused by KVM and passed over; a hypervisor
without the call (Vcpu::pre_fault_memory's default, ENOSYS) stops the thread.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0119BJfiAjA3VRAtdz3uU35x
